  "live_records": {
    "q-102094": {
      "dom_id": "step-q102094",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14240560/assets/1d4b1fa9cd4318ddba32a17ce858f4982cf7151bc5931b4163b2dc0ea6898e46.png)\n\nGiven the sets $A=\\{13,22,29,31,36\\}$ and $B=\\{29,31,36,42,47\\},$ find $A\\cap B.$",
      "worked_solution": "The intersection $A\\cap B$ consists of all the elements that are in both $A$ and $B.$ To represent $A\\cap B,$ we will shade in all the area that is contained in both $A$ and $B.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14240560/assets/0c4523eaa405cb9f2140338beab8ed05e4409b7fafb2bf837ad8c22b985f5c56.png)\n\nThe elements $29,$ $31,$ and $36$ are in both $A$ and $B.$ Therefore, the intersection of $A$ and $B$ is\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=\\{29,31,36\\}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
