    "q-102077": {
      "dom_id": "step-q102077",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14240560/assets/e495695d9def7518780fb2b9e44f11e890da4c70c2dff7c6525936b2684219e1.png)\n\nGiven the sets $S=\\{8,10,11\\}$ and $T=\\{10,12,15\\},$ find $S\\cap T.$",
      "worked_solution": "The intersection $S\\cap T$ consists of all the elements that are in both $S$ and $T.$ To represent $S\\cap T,$ we will shade in all the area that is contained in both $S$ and $T.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14240560/assets/5f807aaf7a026cc52f53f4a5f50afab269da5f2dfdcb835f6166891ada864f72.png)\n\nOnly the element $10$ is in both $S$ and $T.$ Therefore, the intersection of $S$ and $T$ is\n\n$$\nS\\cap T=\\{10\\}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
